**Singleton (Meyers)**:
```cpp
class Config {
 public:
  static Config& instance() { static Config c; return c; }  // thread-safe since C++11
  Config(const Config&) = delete;
  Config& operator=(const Config&) = delete;
 private:
  Config() = default;
};
```
Function-local statics are initialised exactly once and thread-safely. Drawbacks: global state, hidden dependencies, and hard testing.

**`unique_ptr`-like (exclusive ownership)**:
```cpp
template <class T> class UniquePtr {
  T* p_ = nullptr;
 public:
  explicit UniquePtr(T* p = nullptr) : p_(p) {}
  ~UniquePtr() { delete p_; }
  UniquePtr(const UniquePtr&) = delete;
  UniquePtr& operator=(const UniquePtr&) = delete;
  UniquePtr(UniquePtr&& o) noexcept : p_(o.p_) { o.p_ = nullptr; }
  UniquePtr& operator=(UniquePtr&& o) noexcept {
    if (this != &o) { delete p_; p_ = o.p_; o.p_ = nullptr; }
    return *this;
  }
  T& operator*() const { return *p_; }
  T* operator->() const { return p_; }
  T* get() const { return p_; }
  T* release() { T* t = p_; p_ = nullptr; return t; }
  void reset(T* p = nullptr) { delete p_; p_ = p; }
};
```
Copy is deleted, move transfers ownership, and the destructor releases the resource (RAII).

**`shared_ptr`-like (shared ownership)**: keep a heap-allocated control block `{ std::atomic<long> count; }` shared by all owners. Copy: copy the pointers and increment. Destructor: decrement, and when it reaches zero delete the object and the control block. Copy-assign: guard self-assignment (increment the new one first, then release the old). Move: steal both pointers and leave the source null. The count must be atomic so copies on different threads are safe; the pointee itself is not made thread-safe. Real `shared_ptr` also has a custom deleter, `weak_ptr` (weak count to break cycles) and `make_shared` (one allocation).

**Mistakes to avoid**: missing rule of five, double delete, forgetting self-assignment, creating two control blocks from the same raw pointer, and reference cycles (use `weak_ptr`).
